  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe main concern for the forecast period remains the possibility of \nadditional flooding issues overnight and during the day Thursday. As \nof 10 PM EDT on Wed July 22nd, a cold front was situated in the TN \nvalley, stretching southwestward into northern AL and MS. This is \ngoing to interact with a northward surging boundary (possibly a \nstrong sea breeze aided by southerly low level flow on the \nnortheastern periphery of TS Bertha) in the coming hours, kicking \noff more robust shower and thunderstorms from northern AL east-\nnortheastward into the southern TN valley and our western NC \ncounties. Similar to last night, CAM guidance has been insistent on \na fairly narrow swath of higher rainfall amounts with good \nprobabilities of seeing over 5\" of rain. This seems very plausible \ngiven confluent WSW low level flow and the high likelihood of \ntraining convection. Some brief storms in McMinn, Monroe, and \nsouthwest Blount county over the last couple of hours have radar \nestimates in the 2-3\" range already. The Flood Watch that was \nissued for the southern areas seems well warranted and I see no \nreason for changes. In terms of timing, it once again seems like \nan overnight through mid/late morning threat window.\n\nBy late morning the influence of TS Bertha will be weakening, with \nincreasingly northerly surface flow across the CWA and falling upper \nheights as shortwave energy moves in. This should push the front and \nassociated rainfall threat just south of our area by the mid \nafternoon hours Thursday. That said, I wouldn't be surprised to see \nsome lingering heavy rain/flooding threat in our far southeast TN \nand western NC counties well into the afternoon hours before the \nfront is more confidently to our south.\n\nFor Friday onward, the general trend will be a building ridge out \nwest and a deepening eastern CONUS trough. Plenty of shortwave \nactivity moving from the upper midwest towards the southern \nAppalachian region from Friday through next week will result in an \nactive weather pattern and periodic chances for showers and \nthunderstorms in the forecast area. A notable shortwave is seen to \nmove from the Ozarks eastward across TN/KY on Friday, which should \nresult in scattered convection across the area Friday afternoon with \nfairly decent coverage in the mountains thanks to low level westerly \nflow. Otherwise, timing of any one of these shortwaves this weekend \ninto next weekend is difficult at the moment. Suffice it to say that \nthere should be several opportunities for showers over the next week.\n\nTemperatures for Thursday into Saturday look to be a bit below \nnormal, then the remainder of the period will likely see \ntemperatures back closer to seasonal norms.",
